YAAP, or "YAFAP - Yet Another Fifth Ascension Post"

ZeroOne the Elven Wizard

Before this wizard I had played probably a dozen orcish wizards and
before them I had ascended three combat-wombat classes and a rogue.

I was not going for any conducts but artifact wishless. In the midgame
I resisted the temptation to polypile some junk, preserving the
polyless conduct. And being a wizard and having magic resistance right
from the beginning polyselfless was easy, too. Neither did I
reverse-genocide anything. Come to think of it, I believe the celibacy
conduct was kept, too.

I started with a pet kitten I named Jack Bauer, after the TV series
called 24. I soon found out that elves have a much higher power
increase than orcs when their levels increase, which I gladly accepted.
I collected some daggers and built a stack of 11 uncursed +0 ones.

I visited the Oracle and then went to explore the Mines. At some point
I tamed a few dogs or cats and, dancing around a polytrap which was
conveniently located in a corridor, polymorphed them into a mind flayer
and a pit fiend. Jack Bauer became a winged gargoyle. At Mines' End I
was attacked by a mind flayer, losing some memories. Soon after that my
pet mind flayer got itself killed by trying to suck the brains of a
fire elemental, which proved that even though they eat the brains of
other animals, they are really stupid creatures and deserve to be
genocided, so I used the scroll I had found to get rid of 'h'. Yeah, no
one ever thinks about the poor hobbits, but whatcha gonna do? Later at
some point I genocided all 'L' and, sitting on a throne, disenchanters.

I had lots of more or less nice loot and was just under the level of
being burdened, so I carried no pick-axe, and as the Mines' End turned
out to be the Wine Cellar version I ascended back to Mine Town. The
altar was coaligned (chaotic) and I started diluting my potions to make
some holy water. My pets, the pit fiend, mind flayer and winged
gargoyle, had taken care of most of the watch, so I wasn't concerned
about that. What took me by surprise was a djinni that emerged from the
fountain - and that granted me a wish! So I wished for a blessed +2
SDSM and got it.

The pets took care of the shopkeepers in the town, and, when I wasn't
paying attention, the priest too, but luckily I had bought quite a bit
of protection already. I had a few spells, force bolt still being the
only attacking spell. This wasn't changed until much later after I had
done the sokoban and got the luckstone from mines, when I got the
spellbook of finger of death when sacrifing monsters at an altar at
dlvl ~10, sacrificing monsters I kept making using the spell of create
monster. Quite the extreme ends - force bolt and the finger, huh? The
only other attacking spellbook I found in the game was a spellbook of
fire, in Gehennom.

Anyway, Mine Town had become a ghost town so I went to the main dungeon
and to the Sokoban. Luckily I got the bag of holding from there, so put
my items into it and went back to Mines' End to get the luckstone. At
some point I even got Magicbane. The other gifts from Anhur were the
Werebane, the aforementioned Finger and, at the end game, the Frost
Brand. I successfully kept using used all three artifacts. Oh yeah, and
I did name my elven dagger Sting right in the beginning, probably after
a couple of hundred turns.

The quest was easy and after getting the Eye I set up a stash at
Sokoban; I had all my books there. I never casted 'create monster'
because I found it somewhere right at the beginning but wasn't of high
enough level, and didn't carry the book around and just lost it.

All of my pets but Jack Bauer the winged gargoyle died at some point
way before the Castle, and Bauer stepped on a level teleport trap. I
thought I'd never see him again, but I did, at the ascension run. :) I
didn't stop to re-tame him but didn't kill him either.

Acquiring the spellbook of identify when offering corpses was sort of a
turning point, too. I later blanked all my 7 scrolls of identify. I
found the first scroll of sickness, the smoky potion, somewhere past
dlvl 20, blessed it, and got the second wish of in the game. I used it
for an uncursed magic marker. I found another magic marker later very
deep in the gehennom. Most of the charges went to scrolls of enchant
armor or enchant weapon. Although I did write and read one cursed
confused scroll of destroy armor to fooproof some specific piece of
armor I had. I never used the castle wand, which was 0:2.

Gehennom was as boring as always, but the spell of magic mapping made
it easier. I bribed most of the demon lords to avoid the stupid
run-to-the-stairs game. I kept going down and occasionally #invoked the
Eye to bring my loot to the stash at sokoban or to restudy some
spellbooks. I didn't find Fort Ludios, but I didn't search for it
either. I think I looted only one vault in this game.

At Vlad's Tower, whose entrance was at the Baalzebub's Lair level, I
had the worst fat-fingering experience ever, resulting in losing an
amulet of life saving. I tried to loot a chest: (a)pply - Z (key) - . -
(y)es - enter - (l)oot - (o)pen. This sequence of keys was so automated
by my brain me that it came straight from the spine. However, I
apparently didn't tap a-key hard enough -> (Z)ap. The rest of the keys
were, apparently, ignored, as there were no spells on such slots as "."
or "y" but the (i) slot, which I, apparently, unfortunately hit instead
of the o-key had a spell; The Finger of Death. So I tap in the sequence
in less than 0.1 seconds: "Which direction?" Huh? I thought I already
tapped dot, like, "here"? OK, maybe it just didn't get it. Let's tap it
again... "The magical energy gets released! You irradiate yourself..."
Well, I was rather stunned.

In wizard's tower I had some 20 pets as a result of reading a confused
scroll of taming. ;) Didn't get to take them with me anywhere, though,
as the corridors there are too narrow when standing on stairways.

I killed the Wizard of Yendor a total of 7 times - not once did he hit
me as I always gave him the finger. Otherwise the ascension run was a
pain - I thought the mysterious force would never let me out! The main
dungeon, however, went fine.

On the astral plane I materialized close to the leftmost door. I saw by
telepathy that it was guarded by Death and decided to go for it.
Apparently my +6 Frost Brand, +6 Werebane and +3 Magicbane weren't
doing enough damage, as Death managed to drain some 80 hitpoints from
me; I killed him thrice. Luckily the altar he was guarding was the
correct one so I made Anhur happy. Otherwise I might have had to wish
for a helm of opposite alignment.



